{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ks7hRqWr1Cajs3AjiiYXUe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/lb.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Server-side support for the QUIC load-balancer draft\n4\t//! (<https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers/>):\n5\t//! parse the LB-supplied configuration string into a [`Config`],\n6\t//! then install a per-server CID-generation / -verification context\n7\t//! ([`ConnectionIdContext`]) on a [`Quic`] via the connection-ID\n8\t//! callback hook.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! ## API shape\n11\t//!\n12\t//! * [`Config::parse`] takes the LB string as `&str` and returns\n13\t//!   the populated config; the C `(char const* txt, size_t\n14\t//!   txt_length)` pair plus zeroed out-parameter collapse to a\n15\t//!   normal Rust constructor.\n16\t//! * [`Quic::set_lb_cid_config`] applies a `Config` to the QUIC\n17\t//!   context, installing the per-server CID context.\n18\t//! * [`Quic::clear_lb_cid_config`] tears the installed context back\n19\t//!   down (no-op when none is installed).\n20\t//! * [`ConnectionIdContext::generate`] / [`ConnectionIdContext::verify`]\n21\t//!   are the per-CID callback bodies.  They are exposed as inherent methods\n22\t//!   so the eventual [`crate::ConnectionIdCallback`] trait impl can\n23\t//!   delegate to them; the C `void* connection_id_cb_data` parameter is\n24\t//!   recovered as `&mut self`.\n25\t//!\n26\t//! ## Field-shape decisions\n27\t//!\n28\t//! * `rotation_bits : 2` — multi-bit C bitfield, kept as `u8`\n29\t//!   (legal range `0..=3`) per the Phase 1 bitfield rule.\n30\t//! * `first_byte_encodes_length : 1` — single-bit C bitfield used\n31\t//!   as a Boolean flag throughout the C body, promoted to `bool`\n32\t//!   (matching the `config.rs` precedent).\n33\t//! * `cid_encryption_context` / `cid_decryption_context` were\n34\t//!   `void*` in C, holding AES-128-ECB contexts produced by\n35\t//!   `aes128_ecb_create` and freed by `aes128_ecb_free` (both\n36\t//!   declared in `tls_api.h`, which has not been translated yet).\n37\t//!   The fields own their contexts in C (the clear path explicitly\n38\t//!   calls the AES destructor), so Rust models them as\n39\t//!   `Option<Box<Aes128EcbContext>>`, with the AES-context type\n40\t//!   forward-declared here as an opaque struct.  `Box::drop` will\n41\t//!   subsume the explicit `aes128_ecb_free` call once Phase 3 gives\n42\t//!   the type a `Drop` impl.\n43\t//! * `server_id: [u8; 16]` and `cid_encryption_key: [u8; 16]` stay\n44\t//!   as fixed-capacity byte arrays — the C declarations are size-16\n45\t//!   arrays and the algorithms are AES-128, so the capacity *is*\n46\t//!   part of the contract.\n47\t\n48\tuse crate::Error;\n49\tuse crate::tls_api::Aes128EcbContext;\n50\tuse crate::{ConnectionId, Quic};\n51\t\n52\t/// CID-encoding method selected by the load-balancer config.\n53\t/// C: `load_balancer_cid_method_enum`.\n54\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n55\tpub enum ConnectionIdMethod {\n56\t    /// Server ID copied in clear after the first byte.\n57\t    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_clear`.\n58\t    #[default]\n59\t    Clear,\n60\t    /// Server ID encrypted with an AES-128-ECB stream cipher keyed\n61\t    /// off a per-CID nonce.\n62\t    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_stream_cipher`.\n63\t    StreamCipher,\n64\t    /// Server ID encrypted with a single AES-128-ECB block.\n65\t    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_block_cipher`.\n66\t    BlockCipher,\n67\t}\n68\t\n69\t/// Number of low bits of the first CID byte that select between\n70\t/// short-lived configurations.  C: `unsigned int rotation_bits : 2`.\n71\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n72\t#[repr(u8)]\n73\tpub enum RotationBits {\n74\t    #[default]\n75\t    Zero = 0,\n76\t    One = 1,\n77\t    Two = 2,\n78\t    Three = 3,\n79\t}\n80\t\n81\t/// Configuration parsed from an LB-supplied string and applied to\n82\t/// a [`Quic`] via [`Quic::set_lb_cid_config`].\n83\t/// C: `load_balancer_config_t`.\n84\t///\n85\t/// `repr(C)` is dropped — the struct is purely an internal shape,\n86\t/// never inspected by an LB or another process.\n87\t///\n88\t/// Lives in `crate::lb`; consumers that also import the demo-app\n89\t/// [`crate::config::Config`] should rename one at the import\n90\t/// site (`use crate::lb::Config as LbConfig;`).\n91\t#[derive(Debug, Default)]\n92\tpub struct Config {\n93\t    pub method: ConnectionIdMethod,\n94\t    pub rotation_bits: RotationBits,\n95\t    /// 1-bit field in C; promoted to `bool` to match call-site usage.\n96\t    pub first_byte_encodes_length: bool,\n97\t    pub server_id_length: usize,\n98\t    /// Used in stream-cipher mode.\n99\t    pub nonce_length: usize,\n100\t    pub connection_id_length: usize,\n101\t    pub server_id: u64,\n102\t    pub cid_encryption_key: [u8; 16],\n103\t}\n104\t\n105\timpl Config {\n106\t    /// Parse an LB-format configuration string into a fresh\n107\t    /// [`Config`].  Returns `Err` when the string is malformed,\n108\t    /// the embedded server ID overflows 8 bytes, or the encoded CID\n109\t    /// length is incompatible with the chosen method.\n110\t    /// C: `lb_compat_cid_config_parse`.\n111\t    ///\n112\t    /// The C signature took an out-parameter and a `(char const*\n113\t    /// txt, size_t txt_length)` pair returning an `int`; the Rust\n114\t    /// signature returns the populated struct in the success arm\n115\t    /// and folds the explicit length into the `&str` slice.\n116\t    pub fn parse(_txt: &str) -> Result<Self, Error> {\n117\t        todo!()\n118\t    }\n119\t}\n120\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6e0a846-1f78-4cd7-8562-a63ac35b77bc","uuid":"8577f7cd-cac2-4abd-8cde-7f9637add8f5","timestamp":"2026-05-05T11:19:33.442Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/lb.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/lb.h`.\n//!\n//! Server-side support for the QUIC load-balancer draft\n//! (<https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers/>):\n//! parse the LB-supplied configuration string into a [`Config`],\n//! then install a per-server CID-generation / -verification context\n//! ([`ConnectionIdContext`]) on a [`Quic`] via the connection-ID\n//! callback hook.\n//!\n//! ## API shape\n//!\n//! * [`Config::parse`] takes the LB string as `&str` and returns\n//!   the populated config; the C `(char const* txt, size_t\n//!   txt_length)` pair plus zeroed out-parameter collapse to a\n//!   normal Rust constructor.\n//! * [`Quic::set_lb_cid_config`] applies a `Config` to the QUIC\n//!   context, installing the per-server CID context.\n//! * [`Quic::clear_lb_cid_config`] tears the installed context back\n//!   down (no-op when none is installed).\n//! * [`ConnectionIdContext::generate`] / [`ConnectionIdContext::verify`]\n//!   are the per-CID callback bodies.  They are exposed as inherent methods\n//!   so the eventual [`crate::ConnectionIdCallback`] trait impl can\n//!   delegate to them; the C `void* connection_id_cb_data` parameter is\n//!   recovered as `&mut self`.\n//!\n//! ## Field-shape decisions\n//!\n//! * `rotation_bits : 2` — multi-bit C bitfield, kept as `u8`\n//!   (legal range `0..=3`) per the Phase 1 bitfield rule.\n//! * `first_byte_encodes_length : 1` — single-bit C bitfield used\n//!   as a Boolean flag throughout the C body, promoted to `bool`\n//!   (matching the `config.rs` precedent).\n//! * `cid_encryption_context` / `cid_decryption_context` were\n//!   `void*` in C, holding AES-128-ECB contexts produced by\n//!   `aes128_ecb_create` and freed by `aes128_ecb_free` (both\n//!   declared in `tls_api.h`, which has not been translated yet).\n//!   The fields own their contexts in C (the clear path explicitly\n//!   calls the AES destructor), so Rust models them as\n//!   `Option<Box<Aes128EcbContext>>`, with the AES-context type\n//!   forward-declared here as an opaque struct.  `Box::drop` will\n//!   subsume the explicit `aes128_ecb_free` call once Phase 3 gives\n//!   the type a `Drop` impl.\n//! * `server_id: [u8; 16]` and `cid_encryption_key: [u8; 16]` stay\n//!   as fixed-capacity byte arrays — the C declarations are size-16\n//!   arrays and the algorithms are AES-128, so the capacity *is*\n//!   part of the contract.\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::tls_api::Aes128EcbContext;\nuse crate::{ConnectionId, Quic};\n\n/// CID-encoding method selected by the load-balancer config.\n/// C: `load_balancer_cid_method_enum`.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\npub enum ConnectionIdMethod {\n    /// Server ID copied in clear after the first byte.\n    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_clear`.\n    #[default]\n    Clear,\n    /// Server ID encrypted with an AES-128-ECB stream cipher keyed\n    /// off a per-CID nonce.\n    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_stream_cipher`.\n    StreamCipher,\n    /// Server ID encrypted with a single AES-128-ECB block.\n    /// C: `load_balancer_cid_block_cipher`.\n    BlockCipher,\n}\n\n/// Number of low bits of the first CID byte that select between\n/// short-lived configurations.  C: `unsigned int rotation_bits : 2`.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n#[repr(u8)]\npub enum RotationBits {\n    #[default]\n    Zero = 0,\n    One = 1,\n    Two = 2,\n    Three = 3,\n}\n\n/// Configuration parsed from an LB-supplied string and applied to\n/// a [`Quic`] via [`Quic::set_lb_cid_config`].\n/// C: `load_balancer_config_t`.\n///\n/// `repr(C)` is dropped — the struct is purely an internal shape,\n/// never inspected by an LB or another process.\n///\n/// Lives in `crate::lb`; consumers that also import the demo-app\n/// [`crate::config::Config`] should rename one at the import\n/// site (`use crate::lb::Config as LbConfig;`).\n#[derive(Debug, Default)]\npub struct Config {\n    pub method: ConnectionIdMethod,\n    pub rotation_bits: RotationBits,\n    /// 1-bit field in C; promoted to `bool` to match call-site usage.\n    pub first_byte_encodes_length: bool,\n    pub server_id_length: usize,\n    /// Used in stream-cipher mode.\n    pub nonce_length: usize,\n    pub connection_id_length: usize,\n    pub server_id: u64,\n    pub cid_encryption_key: [u8; 16],\n}\n\nimpl Config {\n    /// Parse an LB-format configuration string into a fresh\n    /// [`Config`].  Returns `Err` when the string is malformed,\n    /// the embedded server ID overflows 8 bytes, or the encoded CID\n    /// length is incompatible with the chosen method.\n    /// C: `lb_compat_cid_config_parse`.\n    ///\n    /// The C signature took an out-parameter and a `(char const*\n    /// txt, size_t txt_length)` pair returning an `int`; the Rust\n    /// signature returns the populated struct in the success arm\n    /// and folds the explicit length into the `&str` slice.\n    pub fn parse(_txt: &str) -> Result<Self, Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":214}}}
