{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XVfo8p6TwodYBjKmhFNvw8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/lb.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Server-side support for the QUIC load-balancer draft\n4\t//! (<https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers/>):\n5\t//! parse the LB-supplied configuration string into a [`Config`],\n6\t//! then install a per-server CID-generation / -verification context\n7\t//! ([`ConnectionIdContext`]) on a [`Quic`] via the connection-ID\n8\t//! callback hook.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! ## API shape\n11\t//!\n12\t//! * [`Config::parse`] takes the LB string as `&str` and returns\n13\t//!   the populated config; the C `(char const* txt, size_t\n14\t//!   txt_length)` pair plus zeroed out-parameter collapse to a\n15\t//!   normal Rust constructor.\n16\t//! * [`Quic::set_lb_cid_config`] applies a `Config` to the QUIC\n17\t//!   context, installing the per-server CID context.\n18\t//! * [`Quic::clear_lb_cid_config`] tears the installed context back\n19\t//!   down (no-op when none is installed).\n20\t//! * [`ConnectionIdContext::generate`] / [`ConnectionIdContext::verify`]\n21\t//!   are the per-CID callback bodies.  They are exposed as inherent methods\n22\t//!   so the eventual [`crate::ConnectionIdCallback`] trait impl can\n23\t//!   delegate to them; the C `void* connection_id_cb_data` parameter is\n24\t//!   recovered as `&mut self`.\n25\t//!\n26\t//! ## Field-shape decisions\n27\t//!\n28\t//! * `rotation_bits : 2` — multi-bit C bitfield, kept as `u8`\n29\t//!   (legal range `0..=3`) per the Phase 1 bitfield rule.\n30\t//! * `first_byte_encodes_length : 1` — single-bit C bitfield used\n31\t//!   as a Boolean flag throughout the C body, promoted to `bool`\n32\t//!   (matching the `config.rs` precedent).\n33\t//! * `cid_encryption_context` / `cid_decryption_context` were\n34\t//!   `void*` in C, holding AES-128-ECB contexts produced by\n35\t//!   `aes128_ecb_create` and freed by `aes128_ecb_free